Answer 1 – Linux File System Layout
The server has 50 GB storage and must manage logs, user files, and application data. Logs must not exceed 10 GB, 
  and critical services should not stop because of low disk space.
Simple Partitioning
Partition	Size	Use
/boot	1 GB	Boot files
/	8 GB	Operating system
/var	7 GB	System/application data
/var/log	4 GB	Log files
/home	12 GB	User files
/opt	6 GB	Applications
/tmp	2 GB	Temporary files
swap	4 GB	Virtual memory
Free space	6 GB	Emergency use
Total	50 GB	
Log Rotation
Use logrotate to control log growth:
logrotate /etc/logrotate.conf
It can:
Rotate logs regularly.
Compress old logs.
Delete very old logs.
Prevent logs from continuously increasing.
Disk Monitoring
df -h
Checks available disk space.
du -sh /var/log/*
Checks the size of log files.
Result
The separate /var/log partition prevents logs from filling the entire disk. Logrotate + monitoring + reserved
  free space help keep logs within the required limit and prevent critical services from stopping due to disk exhaustion.

Answer 2 – Kernel-Level Optimization
The Linux system has high CPU usage and frequent context switching. Hardware cannot be upgraded, so the kernel and scheduler
should be optimized. The question specifically asks for scheduler tuning and kernel-module techniques.
Simple Solution
Tune the CPU scheduler
The scheduler decides which process gets CPU time. Adjusting scheduler settings can reduce unnecessary context switching.
Change process priority
nice -n 10 program
This runs a CPU-intensive program with lower priority.
Change priority of a running process
renice 10 -p PID
Use CPU affinity
taskset -c 0,1 program
This allows the process to use selected CPU cores.
Use real-time scheduling
Real-time processes can use scheduling policies such as SCHED_FIFO and SCHED_RR for faster and more predictable processing.
Manage kernel modules
lsmod
modprobe module_name
modprobe -r module_name
Unnecessary modules can be removed to reduce resource usage.
Result
By tuning the scheduler, adjusting process priorities, using CPU affinity, and managing kernel modules, context-switching 
  overhead can be reduced and CPU performance improved. Real-time applications can also receive appropriate priority.

Answer3 – Processing 1 Million Log Entries
The problem requires processing 1 million log entries per day using only shell scripting and completing the task within 2 minutes.
  The solution should use optimized Linux commands.
Simple Solution
Use commands such as awk, grep, sort, uniq, and wc.
The best choice for large log files is awk because it can process the file in a single pass.
Example
To count the total number of log entries:
wc -l server.log
To count error messages:
awk '$2=="ERROR" {count++} END {print count}' server.log
To count different log levels:
awk '{count[$2]++} END {
    for (level in count)
        print level, count[level]
}' server.log
Simple Shell Script
#!/bin/bash
LOGFILE="server.log"
echo "Total entries:"
wc -l < "$LOGFILE"
echo "Log statistics:"
awk '{
    count[$2]++
}
END {
    for (level in count)
        print level, count[level]
}' "$LOGFILE"
Why awk?
Processes large files efficiently.
Reads the file only once.
Avoids unnecessary loops.
Reduces process creation.
Suitable for processing millions of records.
Result
Using awk and other optimized Linux commands allows 1 million log entries to be processed efficiently. A single-pass approach 
reduces processing overhead and helps the script meet the 2-minute requirement, depending on the server hardware and complexity 
of the log processing.

  Answer 4 – Managing a Faulty High-CPU Process
The problem states that a faulty process is consuming 90% CPU. The system must not be restarted, and critical services 
must remain unaffected.
Simple Solution
Step 1: Find the CPU-consuming process
top
or
ps aux --sort=-%cpu | head
These commands show which process is using the most CPU.
Step 2: Identify its PID
Example:
PID    %CPU    COMMAND
2456   90.0    faulty_app
Here, 2456 is the process ID.
Step 3: Monitor the process
top -p 2456
Step 4: Reduce its priority
renice 10 -p 2456
This allows other important processes to get more CPU time.
Step 5: Stop the process safely
First use:
kill -15 2456
If it does not stop, use:
kill -9 2456
Step 6: Check critical services
systemctl status service_name
Make sure critical services are still running.
Result
Using top, ps, renice, and kill, the faulty process can be identified, monitored, and controlled without restarting the system.
  Critical services remain protected while excessive CPU usage is reduced.

  Answer 5 – RAM Allocation for 5 Virtual Machines
The physical server has 16 GB RAM. It must run 5 virtual machines, each requiring at least 2 GB, while the host
operating system requires 4 GB.
Simple Calculation
RAM required by 5 VMs:
5 × 2 GB = 10 GB
RAM required by host OS:
4 GB
Total:
10 GB + 4 GB = 14 GB
Available RAM:
16 GB
Remaining:
16 GB - 14 GB = 2 GB
Proposed Allocation
Resource	RAM
Host OS	4 GB
VM 1	2 GB
VM 2	2 GB
VM 3	2 GB
VM 4	2 GB
VM 5	2 GB
Reserved RAM	2 GB
Total	16 GB
Virtualization Techniques
1. Memory Ballooning:
Reclaims unused memory from VMs when required.
2. Dynamic Allocation:
RAM can be adjusted according to the workload of each VM.
3. Memory Monitoring:
Monitor VM and host memory usage to prevent excessive memory consumption.
Result
All 5 VMs receive their required 2 GB, and the host receives 4 GB. The remaining 2 GB is kept as a safety buffer. 
  Thus, all VMs can operate smoothly within the available 16 GB RAM.
